I don't want this to be misconstrued, because I do appreciate the help.  You did try to guide me in a direction that may work, but I gave up on Window_Command because:
Width and Height broke immediately.  Why?  Oh, because despite defining it, it has it's own functions for width and height:
Code:
Window_Command.prototype.initialize = function(x, y) {
    this.clearCommandList();
    this.makeCommandList();
    var width = this.windowWidth();
    var height = this.windowHeight();
    Window_Selectable.prototype.initialize.call(this, x, y, width, height);
    this.refresh();
    this.select(0);
    this.activate();
};
This is fine, I can change initialize, but it was easier for me to just use .windowWidth() and .windowHeight(), why wouldn't I?  They're alread there.
The one I still don't understand is, it doesn't have it's own x or y, but kept setting x and y to 0 and despite everything I tried it just wouldn't stop doing this.  Window_TitleCommand has an updatePlacement function, but I don't see anything like that in Window_Command, although I guess I could just make one.
It was so frustrating because I was so close to completing the plugin, and where I see the benefits of Window_Command it's like starting right from scratch even though it's an inherited object.
I lost interest in trying to find out why it doesn't work and just decided to use Window_Selectable, grab the indices for the Select() method and create switch cases for 0, 1, 2, etc, whatever I want my menu to input and built my own handler.  I would like to learn how to use Window_Command eventually, but the menu is already completed and everything works exactly as intended.
